  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered thunderstorms, some of which could be\nsevere late today. A few hit-and-miss thunderstorms possible \nagain Friday afternoon. \n\nStill looking at a decent setup for a few strong to severe\nstorms across southern New England today. Modest most unstable CAPE\nvalues projected between 1,000-1,500 J/kg combined with 0-1 km \nstorm relative helicity of 50-100 m2/s2 support this idea. The \nprimary limitation today will be the timing of it all. In other\nwords, can the peak helicity and instability arrive in southern\nNew England around the same time. Still thinking the greatest \nthreat from thunderstorms remains mainly to our west, and\nperhaps as far east as the CT River valley. It appears that the\npeak helicity should arrive after instability starts to wane \nwith sunset. This situation bears monitoring, albeit a level 1 \nof 5 threat. Mostly concerned about damaging straight-line winds\nand perhaps some hail. The potential helicity does not \ncompletely rule out the risk for a tornado either. \n\nThere is also a level 1 of 4 risk for localized flash flooding,\nagain mainly west of the CT River. \n\nA cold front should finally reach southern New England late\ntonight into Friday morning. Until this front completely moves\noffshore some time Friday night, still looking at a risk for\nshowers and possible thunderstorms. Given the much different\ntime of day, not expecting severe weather for Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and seasonable weather follows for\nthis weekend with comfortable humidity.\n\nHigh pressure builds into our region for this weekend. The \nresult will be mainly dry and seasonable weather with\ncomfortable humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A period or two of unsettled weather may return\nearly next week with a backdoor cold front in the vicinity of \nsouthern New England. Large spread/high uncertainty on \ntemperatures, dependent on the location of this front.\n\nStill a highly uncertain forecast for early next week. The key \nsynoptic feature will be the location of a backdoor cold front. \nA slower timing of this front would lead to higher temperatures \nacross eastern MA Monday. This front still appears to be stuck \nsomewhere across eastern MA Tuesday into Wednesday, which should\ndirect a low pressure moving southeast from Canada right across\nour region. While not raining this entire time, a prolonged \nperiod where showers and thunderstorms are possible is expected \nfrom Monday into Wednesday.",
